1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,750
Humans have walked this planet for almost 200,000 years, yet life packed inside a city is only a six to seven thousand year old experiment.

2
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:26,620
More than half of humanity lives in cities today, yet the plain question of which city came first still keeps archaeologists arguing hotly.

3
00:00:26,620 --> 00:00:42,490
Before any crown is handed out, the arena holds three contenders — desert-spring Jericho, roof-walking Çatalhöyük, and between-rivers Uruk.

4
00:00:42,490 --> 00:00:58,080
Last episode we watched why humans invented their first king; today on that same trail we find the first wall, the first kingless city and the first ledger.

5
00:00:58,080 --> 00:01:12,190
Our first stage is the Jordan Valley — a green spring-island blooming in pure desert just north of the Dead Sea, known to history as Jericho.

6
00:01:12,190 --> 00:01:24,710
Close to 9000 BCE — with the wide error bars of that date left alone — hunter-gatherer families settled by the spring and never packed up again.

7
00:01:24,710 --> 00:01:37,750
Mudbrick houses, laid one atop another for centuries, slowly built a settlement mound of more than twenty stacked occupation layers.

8
00:01:37,750 --> 00:01:49,660
An honest first fact of ground: this early settlement was fenced to just 2.4 hectares — no grand metropolis picture at all.

9
00:01:49,660 --> 00:02:03,250
Then this plain spring village pulled off something no world had ever witnessed — a free-standing stone wall with a rising stone tower.

10
00:02:03,250 --> 00:02:16,370
Around the village rose a massive stone perimeter wall — roughly 3.6 metres tall, resting on a base 1.8 metres thick.

11
00:02:16,370 --> 00:02:30,720
Behind it rose something stranger still — a solid round tower about 8.5 metres high, the first monumental building of human history.

12
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:42,070
Inside it wound a stone staircase of exactly 22 steps, climbing along walls coated in fresh plaster.

13
00:02:42,070 --> 00:02:55,510
The labour bill is staggering: nearly 1,000 tons of stone carried up through an estimated 11,000 working-days of shared toil.

14
00:02:55,510 --> 00:03:09,100
The famous pyramids of Egypt rise almost 5,500 years later — by that time this stone tower was already ages old.

15
00:03:09,100 --> 00:03:19,540
Mystery one: no enemy camp is known anywhere near Jericho in that era — so the plain fort theory starts to wobble.

16
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:31,050
Theory two: thickest toward the mudflow side, the wall worked as a flood and debris barrier rather than a shield for war.

17
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:41,330
Theory three falls too: not one burial was found inside — so the claim that the monument was a tomb stands nowhere.

18
00:03:41,330 --> 00:03:53,440
In 2011 researchers showed that at summer-solstice sunset the shadow of nearby Mount Quruntul strikes this very tower first.

19
00:03:53,440 --> 00:04:05,720
Those two researchers read the tower as shared mobilisation — shared fear converted into a tool that binds everyone into organised labour.

20
00:04:05,720 --> 00:04:19,310
Population numbers stay openly swinging: some reconstructions give 400 to 900 residents, while the old Kenyon estimate said 3,000 to 4,000.

21
00:04:19,310 --> 00:04:33,340
Between 1952 and 1958 the British archaeologist Kathleen Kenyon excavated and first bared the true form of wall and tower.

22
00:04:33,340 --> 00:04:42,610
In the Kenyon announcement Jericho carried the proud claim of being the oldest city known in the world.

23
00:04:42,610 --> 00:04:55,810
Modern scholarship has turned the plate: Neolithic Jericho was an enormous village, and a true city there only appears near 3000 BCE.

24
00:04:55,810 --> 00:05:08,080
So before crowning any oldest-city champion, we must first cross a stranger kind of city — one that owned no streets at all.

25
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:20,680
Our second contender rises on the golden Konya plain of Turkey — the twin-peaked mound of Çatalhöyük, a name meaning forked mound.

26
00:05:20,680 --> 00:05:34,110
Mellaart struck the first note in 1958, and digs from 1961 to 1965 lifted an 18-level town back into daylight.

27
00:05:34,110 --> 00:05:47,780
From 7400 down to 6200 BCE — nearly 1,400 years of unbroken human living on that very same mound.

28
00:05:47,780 --> 00:05:59,980
Here stands the strangest truth: this town had no streets — houses stood glued back to back, and rooftops were the roads.

29
00:05:59,980 --> 00:06:11,100
A single house held 5 to 10 people — hearth, mats and grain stores all jammed into that one breathing space.

30
00:06:11,100 --> 00:06:20,890
Visiting a neighbour? Take the wooden ladder — in our city the road means a rooftop, and hearth-smoke tells you whose roof it is.

31
00:06:20,890 --> 00:06:32,000
The dead never left the house — families buried them right beneath the floor the living kept walking on every day.

32
00:06:32,000 --> 00:06:44,120
A single house floor could be freshly plastered up to 450 times — a harvest of care across one long human lifetime.

33
00:06:44,120 --> 00:06:55,160
One house sheltered a family for 45 to 90 years, and rose anew about six times on exactly the same spot.

34
00:06:55,160 --> 00:07:08,990
Across this enormous 13.5 hectare town excavators found no palace, no temple, no wealthy quarter — nothing, nowhere.

35
00:07:08,990 --> 00:07:18,860
Skeleton studies added another firm fact: men and women were eating at almost perfectly equal nutrition levels.

36
00:07:18,860 --> 00:07:29,730
The grand mother-goddess-ruler story of Mellaart collapsed — fresh digging by Hodder from 1993 cancelled that reading.

37
00:07:29,730 --> 00:07:44,320
The headcount refuses one number: older estimates gave 3,500 to 8,000 residents, while a 2024 recount argues only 600 to 800.

38
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:55,520
One 2025 genetic study adds a line: house residence passed from mother to daughter — a matrilineal hint.

39
00:07:55,520 --> 00:08:08,190
After some 1,400 years the residents quietly crossed to the nearby West Mound — why, is still an open file.

40
00:08:08,190 --> 00:08:19,940
Here sits the most impossible truth: a settlement this enormous survived without any crown at all — the deepest lesson of excavation.

41
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:29,850
But the true birthplace of the crown was waiting deeper south — right between the two muddy rivers.

42
00:08:29,850 --> 00:08:38,960
The third contender lies deeper south — the marshy flats of southern Iraq, wedged between two great rivers.

43
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:50,000
Little reed boats travelled along hand-dug canal lines cut through the flat marsh — canals netting the whole plain like a web.

44
00:08:50,000 --> 00:09:00,910
Two old Ubaid villages — Unug and Kullaba — merged into one, and that union took the name Uruk.

45
00:09:00,910 --> 00:09:10,740
Regular floods laid silt so fresh that barley harvests kept growing past what village mouths could swallow.

46
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:24,340
Surplus grain kept pouring year after year into temple storehouses, piling ton upon ton — stores filling every season.

47
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:33,380
The temple priest-stewards kept the daily count and handed the share back out in sanctioned packet sizes.

48
00:09:33,380 --> 00:09:45,170
Temple facades bristled with thousands of coloured clay cones set point-outward, glittering as rainbow zigzag mosaics.

49
00:09:45,170 --> 00:09:57,200
The sky god Anu White Temple was raised near 3300 BCE on a high terrace, all in blinding whitewash.

50
00:09:57,200 --> 00:10:07,870
Beside it lay the vast Eanna precinct of goddess Inanna — a walled ground packed with stores, workshops and scribe tables.

51
00:10:07,870 --> 00:10:18,460
Its Limestone Temple measured 76 by 30 metres — a shell bigger even than the later Parthenon of Athens.

52
00:10:18,460 --> 00:10:30,210
By about 3500 BCE the sprawl of Uruk surged past 100 hectares, and the wave of newcomers refused to stop.

53
00:10:30,210 --> 00:10:40,480
About 50,000 people lived inside the wall, and 80,000 to 90,000 more across the wider network.

54
00:10:40,480 --> 00:10:51,590
And Uruk rose to become the largest living settlement anywhere on Earth in that whole era — a record never seen before.

55
00:10:51,590 --> 00:11:03,340
The city size swung openly too: 250 hectares inside the walled core, up to 450 hectares at its fullest.

56
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:16,460
Then rose a city wall 9 to 9.5 kilometres long, standing 12 to 15 metres high in stamped mudbrick courses.

57
00:11:16,460 --> 00:11:28,740
A later age raised Gilgamesh as king of Uruk — a 27th-century entry in the king list, legend rather than excavation.

58
00:11:28,740 --> 00:11:39,500
The epic sings on — the hero stands tall upon the high rampart of Uruk, the whole city under his gaze.

59
00:11:39,500 --> 00:11:50,250
And archaeology answers: that brick wall truly stood near 3000 BCE — a real wall of a real city.

60
00:11:50,250 --> 00:12:02,440
Climb onto my wall top — from there alone let your eyes measure out my city, only then will you grasp how grand it stands.

61
00:12:02,440 --> 00:12:14,870
Here the episode-one thread completes: surplus grain, temple accounting and an installed king — all inside one wall.

62
00:12:14,870 --> 00:12:26,390
But 50,000 human minds cannot hold a temple ledger in memory — numbers now demanded a new tool.

63
00:12:26,390 --> 00:12:38,740
People had already counted herds and grain with shaped clay tokens for eight thousand years — a counting method that ran for thousands of years.

64
00:12:38,740 --> 00:12:50,340
Around 3500 BCE debt-tokens were shut inside hollow baked-clay balls, verified by the marks pressed outside.

65
00:12:50,340 --> 00:13:02,770
By about 3200 BCE the inner tokens gave way to pressed signs — the first writing was born from accounting itself.

66
00:13:02,770 --> 00:13:15,050
Today tablet: four jars of beer, three measures of wool, and forty sheep — for the temple, and such tablets filled up every single day.

67
00:13:15,050 --> 00:13:28,490
Nearly six thousand tablets from the Eanna precinct all talk accounting — barley, beer, wool, sheep — no poetry at all.

68
00:13:28,490 --> 00:13:38,760
Later scribes learned to write names sound by sound — writing was learning to hold speech itself.

69
00:13:38,760 --> 00:13:52,790
Theory one: river canal irrigation multiplied surplus and tied human labour into one single system, which pulled canal-digging labour under one roof.

70
00:13:52,790 --> 00:14:05,310
Theory two: spreading dryness pushed rain farmers further south into the crowded river plains, and crowds thickened on the fertile southern ground.

71
00:14:05,310 --> 00:14:19,420
Theory three credits trade and markets — but a 2014 analysis proved cities came first, since evidence shows cities arrived before such markets.

72
00:14:19,420 --> 00:14:35,010
Theory four: circumscribed land of rivers, desert and mountains blocks escape, so war squeezes villages into states, where losers found every escape route blocked.

73
00:14:35,010 --> 00:14:52,400
The honest answer stands single: no lone dial did it — surplus, water, climate, trade and fear all turned the wheel together, and every theory-thread knots into that one truth.

74
00:14:52,400 --> 00:15:05,160
Thousands of people packed behind a single wall made the first true disease super-spreaders, and cheek-by-jowl living gave disease its wings.

75
00:15:05,160 --> 00:15:22,880
The earliest written epidemic advice is a Mari letter from about 1775 BCE: do not share cup, chair or bed — and that tablet sent to the Mari court can still be read today.

76
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:36,640
Tooth enamel records show nutrition and health actually dipped in the first proto-state centuries, and bodies paid the first instalment of the city price.

77
00:15:36,640 --> 00:15:49,310
The Algaze paradox states the early cities were entropic: the moment the tide of fresh migrants stopped, they slid toward collapse.

78
00:15:49,310 --> 00:16:03,350
Tell Brak in the north also spread across 130 hectares with its own Eye Temple — proving urbanism was never a one-city miracle.

79
00:16:03,350 --> 00:16:14,790
The northern cities grew by pulling in newcomers, yet separate social lines survived — they lived side by side but never blended.

80
00:16:14,790 --> 00:16:29,060
So the record clarifies: the first city was never one alone — many sprouted in many places with one shared toolbox, drawn from a single shared pool of craft.

81
00:16:29,060 --> 00:16:42,570
So the final tally: Jericho raised the first wall, kingless Çatalhöyük lived on as a city, and Uruk built the first true city.

82
00:16:42,570 --> 00:16:58,970
One guarded note before the crown: the first-city title hinges on how you define city itself — change the definition and the crown moves too, so no single winner can be rushed.

83
00:16:58,970 --> 00:17:15,040
History keeps three firsts running side by side — wall, streetless city, true city — never a lone champion, and that is the safest verdict of excavation.

84
00:17:15,040 --> 00:17:28,470
And their shared gift: the wall, the tax ledger, the epidemic memo — the whole toolkit of civilization, still in our hands.

85
00:17:28,470 --> 00:17:40,500
Which of the three champions convinced you — the Tower, the streetless town, or the walled metropolis? Drop your pick in the comments below.

86
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:55,010
Every number here was checked against UNESCO reports, the Antiquity journal, Britannica and museum handbooks — and where scholars disagree, both views were kept.

87
00:17:55,010 --> 00:18:07,770
Subscribe, so the Time Chronicler keeps knocking at your door of history — every door of the past will keep opening for you.

88
00:18:07,770 --> 00:18:18,890
Next episode: the story of the first language of our species — where tablets of accounting will one day become poetry.
